# Phase 6.3: cached OAuth token from sheets_writer's first-run user-flow.
# Stored next to the script as .sheets_oauth_token.json; gitignored to
# keep per-user credentials out of git.
output/.sheets_oauth_token.json
# MLB-209: the second OAuth profile (the published tool's drive.file
# grant) caches separately, and the ledger of workbooks that profile
# created holds spreadsheet ids -- which for a link-viewable book ARE the
# capability. Both are per-user state, never committed.
output/.sheets_public_oauth_token.json
output/.sheets_public_workbooks.json
#
# BACKING ONE OF THESE UP: keep `.json` LAST. `*.json` matches on the
# final extension, so `.sheets_public_oauth_token.json.bak` is NOT
# ignored -- it lands untracked-but-committable, holding live refresh
# tokens, one `git add .` from disaster. Use
# `.sheets_public_oauth_token.pre-<thing>.json` instead. This was caught
# during MLB-209 before any harm; it is written down so it is not
# re-derived under time pressure.
#
# The MAINTAINER's client JSON still lives outside the repo entirely.
# The PUBLISHED tool's client is different in kind and does ship, as
# `output/public_oauth_client.py`, secret included -- Google's token
# endpoint requires a client secret for that configured Desktop client
# (measured live, MLB-209), and an installed app cannot keep one
# confidential anyway. It is a dedicated, rotatable credential in a Cloud
# project used for nothing else, guarded by a narrow format-scoped
# exception in `.gitleaks.toml`. Tokens are still never committed; a
# client descriptor is not a token.
#
# It is a .py and not a .json on purpose: the blanket `*.json` rule below
# has only four narrow re-includes, none of them `output/`, so a
# descriptor written as JSON there would be silently unshippable --
# `git add` would refuse it and a clone would never receive it, while
# the docs claimed no client was needed.
# Defensive: any private-key file shape, in case one ever ends up
# inside the repo dir by accident. Keys are recommended to live in the
# home directory (see SETUP.md key-pair auth section); these patterns
# are belt-and-suspenders.
*.p8
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*

# Release bundles (MLB-209). `tools/build_release_bundle.py` writes the
# distributable ZIP here, and that ZIP CONTAINS THE PUBLIC OAUTH CLIENT
# SECRET -- injected into a temporary tree, never into tracked source.
# GitHub's partner secret scanning reports supported credentials found in
# public history straight to Google, out of band from any scanner config
# in this repo, so a bundle that got committed could not be un-committed.
# This entry is what keeps `git add -A` from doing it.
dist/

